Create a DynamoDB Table and Perform CRUD
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Run LocalStack (open-source AWS emulator) via Docker Compose
Create a DynamoDB table with a partition key and provisioned throughput
Perform Create, Read, Update, Delete (CRUD) operations using AWS CLI
Query and scan a DynamoDB table
Clean up resources by deleting items and tables
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command-line familiarity
Understanding of NoSQL concepts (tables, keys, items vs. relational rows)
Familiarity with JSON syntax
Docker and Docker Compose installed (or install steps below)
AWS CLI v2 installed (used only to talk to LocalStack, no real AWS account needed)
Environment Setup
You are working on a single Linux machine (provided via Start Lab).

1. Install Docker and Docker Compose (if not present):

sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y docker.io docker-compose-plugin
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER && newgrp docker
2. Install AWS CLI v2:

curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"
unzip awscliv2.zip
sudo ./aws/install
aws --version
3. Configure dummy AWS credentials (required by CLI, not validated by LocalStack):

aws configure set aws_access_key_id test
aws configure set aws_secret_access_key test
aws configure set region us-east-1
Tasks
Task 1: Start LocalStack with DynamoDB Enabled
Create a working directory and a docker-compose.yml file.

mkdir ~/dynamodb-lab && cd ~/dynamodb-lab
nano docker-compose.yml
Complete the file below (TODO items must be filled in):

version: "3.8"
services:
  localstack:
    image: localstack/localstack:latest
    container_name: localstack
    ports:
      - "4566:4566"
    environment:
      # TODO: enable only the dynamodb service
      - SERVICES=
      - DEBUG=1
    volumes:
      - "./volume:/var/lib/localstack"
Start the container:

docker compose up -d
docker ps
Verify DynamoDB endpoint is live:

curl http://localhost:4566/_localstack/health
Note: All subsequent CLI commands need --endpoint-url http://localhost:4566. To simplify, define an alias:

alias ddb="aws --endpoint-url=http://localhost:4566 dynamodb"
Task 2: Create the Products Table
Fill in the missing key schema and throughput values.

ddb create-table \
  --table-name Products \
  --attribute-definitions AttributeName=ProductID,AttributeType=S \
  --key-schema AttributeName=ProductID,KeyType=___ \
  --provisioned-throughput ReadCapacityUnits=___,WriteCapacityUnits=___
Confirm creation:

ddb list-tables
ddb describe-table --table-name Products
Task 3: Insert Sample Items (Create)
Insert three products. Complete the second and third put-item commands based on the pattern of the first.

ddb put-item --table-name Products --item '{
  "ProductID": {"S": "P001"},
  "Name": {"S": "Laptop"},
  "Price": {"N": "899.99"},
  "Stock": {"N": "15"}
}'

# TODO: Insert P002 - "Wireless Mouse", Price 19.99, Stock 100
ddb put-item --table-name Products --item '{ ___ }'

# TODO: Insert P003 - "USB-C Hub", Price 29.50, Stock 40
ddb put-item --table-name Products --item '{ ___ }'
Task 4: Read an Item (Query)
Retrieve a specific product by key using get-item:

ddb get-item --table-name Products --key '{"ProductID": {"S": "P001"}}'
Challenge: Use query (not get-item) to fetch P002, using --key-condition-expression and --expression-attribute-values. Reference the AWS CLI docs for the exact syntax.

ddb query --table-name Products \
  --key-condition-expression "ProductID = :id" \
  --expression-attribute-values '{":id": {"S": "___"}}'
Task 5: Update an Item
Update the price of P001 to 849.99 using update-item. Complete the expression syntax:

ddb update-item --table-name Products \
  --key '{"ProductID": {"S": "P001"}}' \
  --update-expression "SET Price = :newPrice" \
  --expression-attribute-values '{":newPrice": {"N": "___"}}' \
  --return-values ALL_NEW
Verify the update with get-item on P001.

Task 6: Scan the Entire Table
ddb scan --table-name Products
Optional filter challenge: Scan only items where Stock is greater than 20. Look up --filter-expression syntax and complete:

ddb scan --table-name Products \
  --filter-expression "Stock > :minStock" \
  --expression-attribute-values '{":minStock": {"N": "___"}}'
Task 7: Delete an Item and the Table
Delete P003:

ddb delete-item --table-name Products --key '{"ProductID": {"S": "___"}}'
Confirm it's gone (scan or get-item), then delete the table:

ddb delete-table --table-name Products
ddb list-tables
Verification
Confirm lab completion on the same machine:

docker ps shows localstack container running (before cleanup)
ddb list-tables initially returned Products, later returns empty list after Task 7
ddb scan --table-name Products (before deletion) showed 3 items, then 2 after delete
get-item for P001 after Task 5 shows updated price 849.99
No errors returned from any CLI command (check for ResourceNotFoundException if table name is mistyped)
Troubleshooting:

If curl health check fails, run docker logs localstack to check startup errors
If CLI hangs, confirm --endpoint-url is included or alias is active in current shell
"Table not found" errors usually mean the table was created in a different region than configured
Conclusion
In this lab, you deployed a local DynamoDB environment using LocalStack and Docker Compose, avoiding real AWS costs while learning core serverless database skills. You created a Products table with a partition key, performed full CRUD operations (put-item, get-item, query, update-item, scan, delete-item), and cleaned up by deleting the table. These skills map directly to real-world DynamoDB usage on AWS and are foundational for the AWS Certified Developer - Associate certification and Cloud Developer/DBA roles in the GCC and global cloud market.
